ased estimator of $\\mu ,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=\\mu .\n$$\n\nTherefore, since the population mean $\\mu =-3,$ we have that $\\text{E}[\\overset{―}{X}]=-3.$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186412754 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186157355 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "b60ce582-4127-57b8-bba8-04cf17169ca3" :knowledge-point/title "Estimating a Population Mean From the Sample Mean"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "0c9718c7-902f-5345-ad03-482984e9c1fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412695} {:answer/id #uuid "1dd3b9ac-3e11-5981-a4d8-309f740a2b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412700} {:answer/id #uuid "5fbe3e02-0a60-5155-aaa9-1099c2486ac5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412713} {:answer/id #uuid "635832eb-ba37-5e03-b2a0-da170625a38a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "2.0" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412715} {:answer/id #uuid "ea847776-7f1b-5b89-bb47-fd8b7fc0ef23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "1.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412739}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "1dd3b9ac-3e11-5981-a4d8-309f740a2b9a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "0.5" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412700} :answer-field/id #uuid "05770093-228f-5a71-80b2-0c344bf688cf" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186412742}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8d4c868b-7564-5dcc-a2d7-26fd40beb90e" :question/math-academy-id "q-88951" :question/problem "Suppose the following data is sampled from a population. Compute an estimate for the population mean.\n\n$$\n4,\\,-7,\\,6,\\,-3,\\,5,\\,-2\n$$" :question/worked-solution "Suppose ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n}$ is a sequence of random variables representing a random sample of size $n$ drawn from a population with the population mean $\\mu .$ \nThen, the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is given by\n\n$$\n\\overset{―}{X}=\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{n}{X}_{i}.\n$$\n\nNote that:\n\n- $\\overset{―}{X}$ is the mean value of ${X}_{1},{X}_{2},…,{X}_{n},$ while $\\overset{¯}{x}$ is the mean of a specific sample ${x}_{1},{x}_{2},…,{x}_{n},$\n\n- the sample mean $\\overset{―}{X}$ is an estimator of the population mean $\\mu ,$ and\n\n- the value $\\overset{^}{\\mu }=\\overset{¯}{x}$ is an estimate of the population mean $\\mu .$\n\nTherefore, our estimate for the population mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\overset{^}{\\mu } & =\\overset{―}{x} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{6}{x}_{i} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{6}(4-7+6-3+5-2) \\\\  & =\\frac{3}{6} \\\\  & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
